const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"hopes-and-fears","name":"Hopes and fears","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 01","shortDesc":"Participants individually write their hopes and concerns on sticky notes, share them honestly with the group, place them on the board, then read them aloud and cluster the recurring themes.","purpose":"We want to gather a group's attitude towards a project. Hopes uncover expectations about what can be done. Fears uncover doubts about the project at hand. A great activity for getting people to open up and share their feelings about where you're going.","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":["To consult"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"20-45 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Markers or pens, \\nsticky notes","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"","proTip":"","implementationTime":"20-45 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) The group gets 5 minutes to individually write on sticky notes their hopes and concerns before talking about them. Invite participants to be honest and share what they feel. Ask them to use 1 sticky for each thought. They can start with:\\nHope: \\"I hope...\\" or \\"It would be great if...\\"\\nFears: \\"I'm concerned that...\\" or \\"This would be great but...\\"\\n2) Once everyone is finished, ask everyone to place their post-its on the board.\\n3) Read it aloud and identify any emerging themes. \\n4) Cluster the answers by moving them in small groups, circle and title your themes.","budgetAdaptation":"You don't need to print the form; you can simply copy the information onto a sheet of paper that's large enough.","examples":"","requirements":"03_Institutional readiness, including willingness to share control and planners’ trust in citizens should be assessed before launching participation","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"people-competence-readiness-canvas","name":"People & Competence Readiness Canvas","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 02 + online excel","shortDesc":"Municipalities go row by row to mark whether each competence needed for the pilot is available internally or externally, fill in who is responsible and how to reach them, spot missing competences as empty rows, and keep the canvas as a living document updated at key project phases.","purpose":"We want to identify which of the necessary competencies we have internally and acknowledge which ones we should look for externally.","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":["To self-govern","To consult","To co-design"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"5-20 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Without a facilitator","suppliesRequired":"Markers or pens for the printed version, Device and internet connection for the online","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"","proTip":"","implementationTime":"5-20 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"This tool is designed to help municipalities understand whether they have the necessary people and competences to carry out a co-creation pilot. In a first round, go through each row and indicate whether the competence is available internally or externally by ticking the corresponding box. At this stage, it is important to be realistic rather than optimistic, leaving blank any competence that is not clearly available.\\n\\nOnce the first mapping is complete, fill in the “Who” and “Contact” columns for all competences that are available, specifying the person or organisation responsible and how they can be reached. The “Notes” column can be used to clarify roles, highlight limited availability, or add any useful detail. Try, as much as possible, to distribute responsibilities across different people, avoiding overloading a single individual.\\n\\nAfter this step, review the table to identify missing competences, which will appear as rows without any boxes checked. These gaps should be addressed either by strengthening internal capacities or by involving external support. It is then important to contact the people identified in the table to confirm their availability and role in the pilot, ensuring that responsibilities are clearly understood and agreed upon.\\n\\nThe file should be considered a living document and kept updated throughout the process. It can be revised when new people are involved, when roles evolve, or when constraints emerge. Regular updates, especially at key project phases, will help maintain a clear overview of capacities and ensure a smoother implementation of the pilot.","budgetAdaptation":"ND","examples":"","requirements":"03_Institutional readiness, including willingness to share control and planners’ trust in citizens should be assessed before launching participation","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"stakeholder-mapping","name":"Stakeholder mapping","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"01 Technique + Tool 03 + Excel Stakeholders's ID Repository","shortDesc":"We want to map actors according to their influence, power, and relevance in relation to NEB values and local challenges","purpose":"We want to map actors according to their influence, power, and relevance in relation to NEB values and local challenges","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":["To consult","To co-design"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"20-45 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Without a facilitator","suppliesRequired":"","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"","proTip":"","implementationTime":"20-45 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1. Map stakeholders one by one, using either:\\n- the Single Stakeholder Card (printed), especially during a workshop or collaborative session;\\n- the Stakeholder Repository (available in our website), for individual work, desk research or remote contributions.\\nFor each stakeholder, collect the key information in a consistent way. The repository becomes the shared reference document for the project and should be updated progressively as new actors, insights or relationships emerge.\\nThe main elements to record in the repository are:\\n- Code and entity: a unique ID and the name of the stakeholder.\\n- Profile: reference person, location, website or useful contacts.\\n- Connection to local challenges: which challenges the stakeholder is connected to, and why.\\n- Interdependencies: what the stakeholder can give to the project, such as assets, knowledge, resources, access, networks or flows; and what they may take or need from the project, such as support, visibility, improved conditions or future benefits.\\n- Related NEB values: whether the stakeholder is mainly connected to Sustainable, Together, Beautiful, or to more than one value.\\n- Influence and interest: the stakeholder’s level of influence on the area and level of interest in the project.\\n\\n2. Use visualisations to read the stakeholder ecosystem\\nOnce the repository has been filled in, use visual maps to create an overview of the stakeholder ecosystem.\\n- The Power / Interest Matrix positions stakeholders according to their level of influence and interest. It can also show the stakeholder cluster they belong to (for example public institutions, schools, cultural actors, private actors, civic groups, sport, health and care) and their connection to NEB values.\\n- The Stakeholders x NEB Values Venn Diagram places each stakeholder, represented through its cluster icon, inside the value or combination of values it refers to: Together, Beautiful and/or Sustainable. This visualisation helps the group understand whether the stakeholder ecosystem is balanced across the three NEB values, which values are over- or under-represented, and how different types of actors are distributed.\\nUse the maps to identify key allies, missing voices, underrepresented stakeholder categories, potential tensions and actors that should be more actively involved in the next phases of the co-design process.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":"Excel Stakeholders's ID Repository (link)"},{"id":"allegato-single-stakeholder-s-id","name":"Single Stakeholder's ID","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 03 B/N no istruzioni (2 ID in ogni A4)","shortDesc":"NO","purpose":"NO","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":[],"mode":"Offline","duration":"Not specified","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"","proTip":"","implementationTime":"Not specified","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"NO","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"stakeholders-engagement-planning","name":"Stakeholders' engagement planning","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 04","shortDesc":"For a specific event, participants list the groups they want to engage and the allies useful for reaching each one, then sort those allies across the before, during and after phases to plan how and when to build the relationships they need.","purpose":"We want to identify key allies for people's engagement and plan the interaction with them in the different events.","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":["To co-design","To self-govern","To consult"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"1h30 - 2h30","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Without a facilitator","suppliesRequired":"Markers or pens, sticky notes","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Emphasise the importance of having the right mediators and allies on board for the project: this is often the key both to getting lots of people to take part and to ensuring the content is of interest to them.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"1h30 - 2h30","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"The tool can be used to organize stakeholder or allies engagement for specific process events. After selecting the relevant event, begin filling out the first section in the top half of the tool. In the first row, list five or more groups of participants you wish to engage. For each participant group, write, in the line below, one or more allies useful for engaging participants. For each identified ally, specify, in the last line, how might they help reaching out and engaging that specific group.\\nThe second section of the sheet indicates three phases: before, during, and after the event. Here, it is possible to sort the previously identified allies according to when they can contribute to the process and to describe the relationship you need to build with them.\\nA digital version of the tool is available for detailed planning of all process events.","budgetAdaptation":"ND","examples":"","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":"Excel (link)"},{"id":"levels-of-participation-map","name":"Levels of Participation Map","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 05","shortDesc":"Participants map, for each level of participation (to self-govern, to co-develop, to consult), the stakeholders involved and the specific aspects or inputs they will be engaged in, updating it from the framing phase through implementation and maintenance.","purpose":"We want to define the extent to which different stakeholders should be involved in decision-making.","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Markers or pens, sticky notes","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Depending on the group you \\ncan decide how to guide \\nthe conversation:\\nFree conversation \\nRound robin\\nThink > Write > Share\\n\\nFor this activity, the presence of a \\ndecision-maker in the room is important, \\notherwise, local partners will need to ask \\nfor confirmation. We recommend \\nwriting down the stakeholders names on \\nsticky notes so that you can reposition them \\nat different levels of involvement during \\ndiscussions with decision-maker and local partners.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"You can either write on it or use sticky notes\\n\\nThe first row of the table refers to the framing and readiness process, prior to development of any future outputs.\\nWork for each participation level (to self-govern, to co-develop, to consult).\\nList stakeholders related to such level, then, identify the specific aspects, parts or inputs they will be engaged in.\\n\\nThe second row of the table refers to the implementation and\\nmaintenance phase. You will update it after co-design events.","budgetAdaptation":"ND","examples":"","requirements":"03_Institutional readiness, including willingness to share control and planners’ trust in citizens should be assessed before launching participation","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"participation-enablers-map","name":"Participation Enablers Map","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 06","shortDesc":"Agreeing on one profile of the people they want to engage, participants map the barriers to participation and that group's desires, needs and goals, then brainstorm ways to overcome the most critical barriers and what the process can offer in return.","purpose":"We want to aknowledge local barriers to participation and identify possible solutions/incentives to make participation easy, frictionless and appealing","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Markers or pens, \\nsticky notes","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Depending on the group you \\ncan decide how to guide \\nthe conversation:\\nFree conversation \\nRound robin\\nThink > Write > Share","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"The group agrees on 1 profile that represents the group of people they wish to engage during the participatory process events.\\n1) In the 1st area, list all the barriers to engaging with your participatory process for each profile. If you have many, clusterize and vote the most critical.\\n2) Then list all the desires, needs or goals they might have as individuals in the upper right area (e.g. do they want to develop new skills, learn a language or find a job?).\\n3) Look at the most critical barriers and brainstorm ways to overcome them, list them in the area below the barriers.\\n4) Look at the needs and desires and brainstorm what the process can offer to help people achieve them while participating.","budgetAdaptation":"ND","examples":"","requirements":"03_Institutional readiness, including willingness to share control and planners’ trust in citizens should be assessed before launching participation","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"neb-adapted-swot-analysis","name":"NEB adapted Swot analysis","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 07","shortDesc":"Working individually or in small groups, participants read a place through the three NEB dimensions (Together, Beautiful, Sustainable), noting for each what might help or harm the transformation, then compare the rows to surface recurring themes, tensions and priorities that guide design.","purpose":"We want to collect and comapre environmental, social, and aesthetic dimensions with traditional strategic analysis.","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"20-45 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Without a facilitator","suppliesRequired":"Markers or pens, sticky notes","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"As facilitator you should help participants move from general impressions to concrete, evidence-based observations. Encourage them to refer to what they have seen, heard or documented, rather than writing generic statements. For example, “elderly people already use the square in the morning” is more useful than “community presence”, and “there is no shaded seating along the main path” is more useful than “lack of comfort”. \\n\\nAsk follow-up questions such as: Who is affected? Where does this happen? When does it happen? What evidence do we have? Why does it matter for the project? \\n\\nHelp the group distinguish between facts, interpretations and assumptions, and make sure that weaker or less visible perspectives are not overlooked.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"20-45 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"Use the NEB-based SWOT Analysis to read a place through the three NEB dimensions: Together, Beautiful and Sustainable, and identify what can support or weaken the transformation process.\\n\\n1) Start from a specific site, challenge or project area. Working individually or in small groups, participants collect evidence-based observations from previous activities or research. For each NEB dimension, they fill in two columns:\\n\\n- What might help captures existing assets, positive conditions, resources, behaviours, relationships or opportunities that can support the project.\\n\\n- What might harm captures barriers, risks, conflicts, exclusions, neglected aspects or weaknesses that may limit the quality, inclusiveness or sustainability of the intervention.\\n\\n2) At the end, compare the three rows to identify recurring themes, tensions and priorities. The output can be used to define design challenges, guide co-design activities, support decision-making or prepare a more strategic action plan.","budgetAdaptation":"ND","examples":"","requirements":"06_Contextual adaptation should be a core principle of the toolkit, aknowledging expectations and participation limits","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"challenge-reframing","name":"Challenge reframing","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"02 Technique","shortDesc":"After earlier framing activities, the facilitator recaps key points and the group revisits the stated challenge, rewriting it around the process with a guided formula: the objective, who participation is made meaningful for, the allies and triggers, and the benefits participants might gain.","purpose":"We want to add insightful details to the initial challenge, including the point of view of the participants.","phase":1,"phaseName":"Framing & Readiness","objectiveTags":["To consult","To co-design"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"5-20 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"1/+10","suppliesRequired":"Markers or pens, \\nsticky notes","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"ND","proTip":"","implementationTime":"5-20 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"This activity is to be used after other framing and readiness activities have been performed.\\nThe facilitator summarizes key points from previous framing activities\\nThe goup checks the challenge previously stated\\nFollowing the process, rewrite the challenge focusing on the process: \\nWe want to build a participatory process to… (key objective)\\nmaking participation meaningful for … thanks to…\\n(allies, triggers, incentives)\\nso that…\\n(benefits they might gain)","budgetAdaptation":"You don’t need to print anythink for this activity, \\njust provide the group with an A3 and a marker.","examples":"We want to build a participatory process to renovate st Paul’s Park, making participation meaningful for young generations so that they can gain new knowledge on green technologies for their future. \\nWe want to build a participatory process to animate the area around the square, making participation meaningful for resident families with young children, so that they make their chores done while participating in events.\\nWe want to build a participatory process to co-design climate-adaptive solutions for the neighborhood, making participation meaningful for local small business owners, so that they can improve the attractiveness and resilience of their businesses while contributing to the public space.\\nWe want to build a participatory process to rethink pathways in the park, making participation meaningful for women and girls, so that they can feel safer and more comfortable in the future design of the space.","requirements":"01_The scope of influence, decision rules, and use of inputs should be explicitly defined and transparently communicated","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"key-informant-interviews","name":"Key informant interviews","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 08","shortDesc":"Starting from a relevant stakeholder, participants select up to five question packs, build a flexible semi-structured interview from warm-up to future-oriented questions, follow the conversation as it opens up, and transfer the key insights into the stakeholder repository or NEB SWOT.","purpose":"We want to collect expert and local knowledge to understand the context, tensions, actors, needs and opportunities before engaging wider groups.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":[],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Without a facilitator","suppliesRequired":"","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"ND","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Start from the stakeholder you want to interview and clarify why they are relevant to the local challenge.\\n2) Select up to 5 question packs from the list, choosing the most relevant ones according to the stakeholder’s role, knowledge and connection to the area.\\nMix question packs from different topic families if useful: access and participation, policies and future plans, or socio-economic trajectories.\\n3) Use the selected questions to build a flexible semi-structured interview: begin with a warm-up question, follow the selected packs, and close with a future-oriented question.\\n4) During the interview, use the main questions as a guide, but follow the conversation they might open.\\n5) After the interview, transfer the key insights into the stakeholder repository or the NEB SWOT Analysis.\\n\\nThe tool should remain flexible: questions can be adapted, skipped or rephrased depending on the conversation and stakeholder profile.","budgetAdaptation":"ND","examples":"","requirements":"05_Local needs, everyday spatial knowledge, and community assets should be identified from the beginning","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"opportunity-map","name":"Opportunity map","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 09","shortDesc":"Participants start from a central challenge, map needs, assets, barriers, ideas and resources around it, connect related elements, cluster them into opportunity areas, and translate the most promising ones into future headlines and first actions.","purpose":"We want to collectively explore a challenge and map the needs, resources, ideas and possible directions that emerge around it, turning scattered inputs into a shared agenda for action.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"20-45 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Can be printed in different formats of as a big poster to allow many people to fill it","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Elements that people might map:\\n- People’s needs\\n- Existing assets\\n- Barriers and tensions\\n- Ideas and possibilities\\n- Potential allies\\n- Resources and constraints","proTip":"","implementationTime":"20-45 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Write the initial challenge or key statement at the centre of a large canvas. This can come from the local diagnostic, SWOT analysis, stakeholder interviews or a previous workshop. \\n2) Around the central statement, invite participants to map different types of inputs: people’s needs, existing assets, barriers, tensions, ideas, potential allies, resources, risks and open questions. \\n3) Ask participants to connect related elements, showing where needs, ideas and resources reinforce each other or where conflicts emerge. \\n4) Cluster the map into opportunity areas.\\n5) For each opportunity area, ask the group to write a short “future headline”: a newspaper-style title that describes the positive change that could happen if this opportunity were realised. This artistic input helps participants move from analysis to imagination. \\n6) Close by selecting the most relevant opportunities and translating them into first actions, responsibilities or questions to explore further. The map can become a shared agenda for governance, stewardship and after-use.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"08_Participation should start before key decisions are made and allow citizens to influence direction rather than react to predefined solutions.","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"world-cafe","name":"World Café","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"03 Technique","shortDesc":"Participants rotate across small café-style tables, discussing focused questions in rounds, while table hosts carry insights forward and the facilitator clusters the results into shared themes, tensions and opportunities.","purpose":"We want to create structured conversations among selected stakeholder groups to build a shared view of challenges, needs and priorities.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To consult"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"1h30 - 2h30","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Tables, chairs, printed questions, paper tablecloths or templates, markers, post-its, name tags.","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Keep questions open but specific. Use table hosts who can listen and summarise without dominating. Make sure quieter voices are invited to contribute. Use visual templates to make conversations concrete.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"1h30 - 2h30","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Set up the room like an informal café, with small tables, background music, snacks and drinks if possible. Cover each table with large paper sheets so participants can freely write, draw and connect ideas. Each table hosts a small group, usually 4–5 people, and one “table host” who remains at the same table throughout the activity.\\n2) The discussion is organised in 3–4 rounds of about 20–30 minutes. In each round, all tables discuss the same question at the same time. At the end of the round, a signal marks the rotation: participants move to different tables and mix with new people, while the host stays and briefly summarises the previous conversation for the new group.\\n3) After each rotation, a new question is introduced. The questions should be designed as a progression, moving from more general reflection to more specific priorities or opportunities. For example, the first question can explore the current situation, the second can focus on needs and tensions, the third on opportunities, and the final one on possible actions or priorities.\\n4) At the end, each host shares the main insights from their table with the whole group, and the facilitator clusters the results into shared themes, tensions, values and opportunities.","budgetAdaptation":"With higer budget you can provide graphic facilitation","examples":"In line with New Europea Bauhaus values, the tables clould cover the tree values of NEB compass. \\nBeauty\\nWhat makes this place meaningful, welcoming or pleasant today?\\nWhat should be protected because it is part of the identity, memory or atmosphere of the place?\\nWhat would make people feel proud, curious or emotionally connected to this space?\\n\\nSustainability\\nWhat environmental challenges are most visible or urgent here?\\nHow could this place better support climate resilience, biodiversity, shade, water management or circular use of resources?\\nWhat existing natural or material resources could be reused, restored or strengthened?\\n\\nTogether\\nWho uses this place today, and who is missing or excluded?\\nWhat barriers prevent people from feeling welcome, safe or able to participate?\\nWhat forms of care, collaboration or shared responsibility could help keep this place alive over time?","requirements":"09_Participatory moments should be high-impact and clearly purposeful","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"open-space-technology","name":"Open Space Technology","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"04 Technique","shortDesc":"Participants create their own discussion agenda around a broad framing question, join or move between conversations freely, and share key takeaways that are then clustered into collective priorities and next steps.","purpose":"We want to let participants define the most relevant issues themselves and self-organise discussions around what matters to them.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To self-govern"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"1h30 - 2h30","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Without a facilitator","suppliesRequired":"Large wall or board, blank agenda grid, paper sheets, markers, post-its, documentation templates.","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Works best when the topic is complex and participants have enough energy and agency. Be clear that not every idea can become a decision. Use the “law of two feet”: people can move where they feel they can contribute or learn.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"1h30 - 2h30","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Start with a broad framing question, such as: “What do we need to understand before transforming this place?” \\n2) Participants propose discussion topics and place them on a shared agenda. \\n3) Each person chooses which conversation to join and can move between groups. The person who proposed the topic records key points, questions, tensions and possible next steps in a short report. \\n4) At the end, all groups share their main takeaways and the facilitator clusters them into collective themes.","budgetAdaptation":"Low budget: community hall, wall agenda and paper notes. \\nMedium/high budget: longer event with documentation team, facilitators, catering and digital reporting.","examples":"Add picture or scheme of the shared agenda","requirements":"09_Participatory moments should be high-impact and clearly purposeful","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"roleplay-stepping-in-others-shoes","name":"Roleplay: stepping in others’ shoes","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"05 Technique","shortDesc":"Participants use character cards to voice needs and tensions around the place. The facilitator (as mayor) maps common ground and conflicts. Session closes identifying 5+ synergies and conflicts to resolve.","purpose":"We want to help participants experience different perspectives and reveal needs, conflicts and barriers that are not immediately visible.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Role cards (printed or handwritten), one per participant or group\\nMarkers or pens\\nSticky notes\\nA shared board or flipchart for clustering debrief insights\\nOptional: printed scenario map or photos of the place","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"1) Avoid assigning roles that are too close to participants' real identities, as this can reinforce rather than shift perspectives.\\n2) A list of possible characters could be: a private investor, a local NGO, a citizen living nearby, a kid, a teenager, and if needed \\n3) some non-humans like a stray cat, a tree…\\n4) If conflict arises during the roleplay, let it play out briefly; tension between characters is often the most generative moment.\\n5) If there is time, before the convergence, swap roles and repeat a shorter round to deepen cross-perspective understanding.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"To prepare in advance: A set of characters’ cards describing a specific user, stakeholder, or affected party (with the role and brief description).\\n\\n1) Introduce the scenario to the group: the facilitator is the mayor and they are gathering to discuss needs and possible synergies for the place. \\n2) Distribute the role cards, one per participant or small group. Give everyone 5 minutes to read and internalize their character silently.\\n3) The roleplay starts: everybody acts as their character presenting their needs and frustrations about the place. The facilitator takes notes on a board noting down when common needs or conflicts emerge. \\n4) Converge: after this round participants are asked to list at least 5 potential synergies or common needs and any conflicts that needs to be solved, still acting as their characters.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"In a co-design process for a small public park, participants were assigned the roles of a parent with a stroller, a teenager, an elderly person with mobility issues, and an old oak tree. During the role-play, it became clear that the main entrance was effectively inaccessible to two of the four characters. Furthermore, since there were few trees around, shade was scarce, and the oak tree suffered from the limited space available. After the role-play, the characters were represented on a sheet of paper and connected to each other using lines of different colors based on the synergies or conflicts identified during the role-play. Finally, the key synergies (the parent with the stroller and the elderly person taking shelter in the shade of the oak tree) and the key conflicts (the oak tree's roots making the ground uneven for the stroller, the teenager listening to loud music and disturbing the elderly person) were noted.","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"walkshop","name":"Walkshop","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"06 Technique","shortDesc":"Participants walk through the site, observe and record perceptions, emotions, barriers and sensory impressions in real time, then collectively translate the findings into spatial insights and design questions.","purpose":"We want to explore the site collectively and capture how people perceive, feel and interpret different places in real time.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"1h30 - 2h30","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Printed maps of the area, preferably one per participant or small group\\nRoute plan, including an accessible route option\\nLarge synthesis map for the final discussion\\nClipboards or hard writing surfaces\\nPens, markers and pencils\\nOptional: Stickers or coloured dots to mark emotions, barriers, opportunities and key points\\nOptional: Phones or cameras for taking photos\\nOptional: Audio recorder or phones for collecting voice notes and sounds, if relevant\\nOptional: digital tool or app for collecting geolocated comments, photos, voice notes and emotional markers\\nOptional: artistic collection tools, such as small bags/envelopes for material samples, texture rubbing sheets, or sensory prompt cards","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"The Actant Journey Map tool could be a way to participate for people that cannot join the walk.\\nThe activity works best in small groups of 3–5 people, with individual moments of observation. \\nAt each stop, use simple prompts to guide observation and discussion: What feels welcoming or uncomfortable here? Who uses this place? Who is missing? What barriers are visible? What natural or material elements shape the experience? What should be protected, repaired or transformed?","proTip":"","implementationTime":"1h30 - 2h30","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Prepare one or more walking route across the site. If possible, prepare an accessible route as well. \\n2) At each stop, ask participants to first observe the place silently for a few minutes and record their own perceptions. Then invite them to share and compare what they noticed with the group. \\n3) During the walk, participants use a printed map or a digital tool to record their perceptions in real time. They can mark points, routes, emotions, barriers, comments and questions. \\n4) Invite participants to collect sensory inputs as well: sounds, textures, smells, colours, memories, atmospheres or small stories connected to specific places. You might use Observarion prompt cards\\n5) After the walk, bring the group together to review the collected material. \\n6) Close the activity by translating the findings into spatial insights and design questions for the next phase of the co-design process.","budgetAdaptation":"Low budget: Use printed maps, pens, stickers and participants’ own phones to collect photos, notes and observations. Facilitation can be kept simple, focusing on guided observation and group discussion.\\nHigh budget: Use a dedicated digital platform or app, live geolocation, emotional heatmaps, professional documentation, enhanced accessibility support and artistic facilation methods to collect sounds, textures, smells and memories. This enables richer data collection and more advanced analysis and visualisation.","examples":"","requirements":"05_Local needs, everyday spatial knowledge, and community assets should be identified from the beginning","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"collective-actant-mapping","name":"Collective actant mapping","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 10","shortDesc":"Participants map the human, non-human, institutional and material actors shaping a place, position them by relevance, connect their relationships, and identify what must be considered in future decisions.","purpose":"We want to understand the human, non-human, material and institutional actors that shape the place and its transformation.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Can be printed in different formats of as a big poster to allow many people to fill it","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"To make the relationships easier to read, use a simple visual legend. For example:\\nRed line = conflict or tension\\nGreen line = care, support or mutual benefit\\nBlue line = dependency\\nBlack arrow = influence or decision-making power\\nDashed line = weak, missing or potential relationship","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Start by writing the place or challenge at the centre of the canvas. This should be the current issue you want to understand before defining any solution.\\n2) Invite participants to identify the actants that currently shape the place and the challenge. Ask them to think broadly, including: users and everyday presences; non-users and missing voices; community actors, institutions and informal power; nature, material elements and more-than-human actors.\\n3) Place each actant in the most relevant quadrant. Then decide how close it should be to the centre: actants directly connected to the challenge go closer to the centre, while those indirectly connected or potentially relevant in the future go further out.\\n4) Once the map is populated, ask participants to draw connections between actants. These can show dependency, conflict, care, influence, exclusion, responsibility or mutual support.\\n5) Use the final part of the canvas to synthesise the discussion: who or what shapes the challenge today, who or what is missing or overlooked, and who or what must be considered in future decisions. This synthesis can inform the next steps of the participatory process, helping define who should be involved, what relationships matter, and what ecological or governance dimensions should not be ignored.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"actant-journey-map","name":"Actant Journey Map","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 11","shortDesc":"Participants follow the experience of a selected user, non-user or non-human actor across a map of the site, tracing barriers, emotions, conflicts and opportunities to translate them into design insights.","purpose":"We want to understand how different people experience the place over time, across routes, touchpoints, emotions and barriers.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Printed base map of the area\\nTransparent tracing sheets, one per participant or group, same fimesin of the map\\nExtra printed copies of the base map, in case tracing sheets are not available\\nCharacter or actant cards, including users, non-users and non-human actors\\nSmall props, tokens or meeples to move across the map\\nColoured markers or pens to trace different journeys\\nSticky notes, preferably in different colours\\nOptional: Small stickers or symbols to mark key moments","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Since participants are “playing” a character, the facilitator should clarify that this is not about inventing stereotypes. The role cards should be based as much as possible on previous research, interviews, observations or local knowledge. When something is imagined, it should be marked as an assumption to verify.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Prepare a printed base map of the area and a set of character cards, including different users, non-users and non-human actors. Each participant or small group chooses one actant.\\n2) Before starting, ask each group to define a specific scenario for their actant. This may include the time of day or year, weather conditions, the reason for being in the area, or the situation they are experiencing. \\n3) Place a transparent tracing sheet over the base map for each actant. Using a small prop, token or meeple, participants move their actant through the space while narrating the experience from that point of view: what the actant sees, does, needs, avoids and feels. As they move, they trace the journey on the transparent sheet, using colours or line styles to distinguish different paths.\\n4) Along the path, participants add notes at key moments. These notes can refer to relevant touchpoints, emotions, barriers, conflicts, resources, questions or opportunities.\\n5) At the end, layer all tracing sheets over the same base map and compare the journeys. Discuss where routes overlap, diverge or conflict, which areas are avoided, which places create positive or negative experiences, and how the same space may be comfortable for one actant but inaccessible, unsafe or unpleasant for another.\\n6) Close the activity by translating the mapped journeys into design insights. Identify critical barriers, emotional hotspots, underused opportunities, conflicting needs and design requirements to inform the next phase of the co-design process.","budgetAdaptation":"If transparent tracing sheets are not available, each participant or group can work on a separate printed copy of the same base map. At the end of the activity, the different maps can be compared side by side, or the facilitator can use a new blank base map to bring together the main routes, barriers, opportunities and key insights that emerged from each journey.","examples":"","requirements":"05_Local needs, everyday spatial knowledge, and community assets should be identified from the beginning","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"observation-prompt-card","name":"Observation prompt card","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"TOOL 12  + 9 carte B/N","shortDesc":"Participants use prompt cards during a walkshop or journey mapping activity to observe the place through different NEB lenses, document what they notice on site, and compare findings afterwards to identify issues, values and opportunities.","purpose":"We want to help participants observe the place through the New European Bauhaus values, capturing sensory qualities, ecological conditions, social dynamics, barriers and signs of care directly on site.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"Not specified","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"","proTip":"","implementationTime":"Not specified","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"Observation propt cards can be added to the walkshop or to the actant journey mapping.\\n1) At the beginning of the activity, give each participant or small group one or more cards. The facilitator can either assign specific cards to different groups, ask participants to choose one, or invite them to draw a card at each stop along the route or during the map exploration. This helps diversify the observations and ensures that the same place is read through different lenses.\\n2) Participants use the cards to guide what they notice, sense and document on site or imagine to be there. The cards should help them observe and think about sensory qualities, social interactions, accessibility barriers, ecological conditions, signs of care or neglect, hidden resources and patterns of use.\\n3) Use the cards again during the synthesis phase. Ask participants to share what they observed through each NEB lens and compare the results. This can help identify emotional hotspots, environmental issues, inclusion barriers, places of attachment, opportunities for care and tensions between different uses or values.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"the-empathy-map","name":"The empathy map","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 13","shortDesc":"Participants use qualitative research to map what a key actant says, thinks, does and feels, then identify contradictions, patterns and critical needs to inform the co-design phase.","purpose":"We want to understand what different users say, think, feel and do in relation to the space, including needs, fears and motivations.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To consult"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"1/+10","suppliesRequired":"Printed or hand-drawn Empathy Map canvas (one per actant profile explored), A2 or larger\\nMarkers or pens\\nSticky notes (ideally in different colours per quadrant)\\nReference material from previous research (interview summaries, observation notes, photos)","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Encourage participants to fill quadrants based on real observations and research, not assumptions. If a quadrant stays empty, treat it as a signal that more research is needed on that dimension.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Gather any available qualitative research about the key actant you want to analyse: observation notes, interview quotes, walkshop recordings, or outputs from previous activities (e.g. Key Informant Interviews, Hopes & Fears).\\n2) Introduce the actant profile with a short description (name, age, role in relation to the space...). If personas have already been developed in a previous activity, use those as the starting point.\\n3) Use a canvas for each profile and work quadrant by quadrant. For each, ask the group to write their observations on sticky notes and place them in the relevant section:\\n- Says: What does this person say out loud about the space? Use direct quotes where possible.\\n- Thinks: What is on their mind but they may not express openly? What concerns or questions do they carry?\\n- Does: What actions do they take in and around the space? What are their routines and behaviours?\\n- Feels: What emotional states does this person experience in relation to the space? Use adjectives + a short context (e.g. \\"Frustrated: no benches to rest on the main route\\").\\n4) Once all quadrants are filled,  look for contradictions, gaps, or patterns across quadrants: these often reveal the most valuable design insights.\\n5) Close with a synthesis: identify the 2-3 most critical needs or tensions emerging from the map. These become key inputs for the co-design phase.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"05_Local needs, everyday spatial knowledge, and community assets should be identified from the beginning","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"personas-non-human-personas","name":"Personas / Non Human personas","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"07 Technique + Tool 14","shortDesc":"Participants create research-based profiles of human and non-human actors connected to the place, using them as reference points to keep diverse needs, barriers and perspectives visible throughout the process.","purpose":"We want to synthesise research into clear profiles that represent different human and non-human needs, behaviours and relationships with the place.","phase":2,"phaseName":"Collective Understanding","objectiveTags":["To consult"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"1/+10","suppliesRequired":"Printed or blank A4 persona card templates (one per persona)\\nMarkers or pens\\nSticky notes\\nReference materials: ecological data sheets, community research summaries, maps, photos of the place and its species","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"Introduce the concept of non-human personas explicitly and early. Many participants may find it unusual at first, so frame it as a way of giving a voice to parts of the ecosystem that cannot speak for themselves in the room, such as trees, soil, water, animals, insects or climate conditions.\\nEncourage participants to use scientific sources, local ecological knowledge or input from environmental experts when filling out non-human profiles. These personas should be grounded in evidence, not purely imagined. When information is uncertain, mark it clearly as an assumption to verify later.\\nFor human personas, avoid creating stereotypes or generic user profiles. Push the group to add nuance, contradictions and specific daily-life details. For example, instead of “elderly woman who needs benches”, write “she crosses the park at 7 a.m. because it is the only quiet moment before the market becomes crowded, but she avoids the central path because the pavement feels unstable after her fall last year.”\\nPersonas should help the group keep different needs, vulnerabilities and relationships with the place visible throughout the process. They should be treated as living synthesis tools, not fixed labels.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Before the session, collect any available research on the place: observation notes, interview summaries, mapping data, ecological reports, or community input gathered in previous activities.\\n2) Define the range of personas to create. Aim for a balanced mix of human profiles, including current users, non-users or people who may feel excluded from the place, and non-human actors. Human personas may include, for example, a regular park user, an elderly resident, a teenager, a parent with a child, a commuter or a person with reduced mobility. Non-human personas may include a mature tree, a local bird species, a canal, the soil ecosystem, rainwater or a stray cat.\\n3) For each persona, fill in a structured profile card. This include: \\nIdentity: name, age or life stage, habitat, and relationship with the place.\\nShort quote: one first-person sentence capturing their point of view on the space.\\nRoutine: when and how they use or inhabit the space, what they do, sense, or contribute.\\nRelationships: who matters most, who shares the space, what is supportive, where tensions exist.\\nNeeds: practical/ecological needs and emotional or symbolic ones.\\nVulnerabilities: what makes the space difficult, unsafe, or inaccessible for them.\\nNEB lens: complete the three closing prompts\\n4) Share the completed personas with the broader group in a plenary session. Use them as reference points throughout subsequent activities, such as Spatial Journey Mapping, Walkshops, Opportunity Mapping or Co-design sessions, to keep both human and non-human needs visible in the process.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"Human persona: \\"Amara, 72, retired seamstress. She crosses the park every morning to reach the market. She needs shaded benches to rest and is afraid of uneven paving after a fall last year. A transformed park with smooth, well-lit paths and greenery would give her back a sense of belonging.\\"\\nNon-human persona: \\"The Old Oak, ~120 years old. My roots stabilise the slope and my canopy shelters 14 bird species. I need at least 15 metres of undisturbed soil around my base. Construction machinery within 10 metres would be life-threatening. A good outcome: I remain as a landmark and my presence becomes a design asset.\\"\\nNon-human persona (ecosystem): \\"The Urban Stream beneath Via Rossini. I am partially culverted and polluted by runoff. I need de-sealing and riparian vegetation to recover. I provide natural cooling and water absorption, uncovering me would benefit both humans and biodiversity.\\"","requirements":"06_Contextual adaptation should be a core principle of the toolkit, aknowledging expectations and participation limits","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"scenario-building","name":"Scenario building","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"Tool 15","shortDesc":"Working from a central question and time horizon, participants refine driver cards and plot them on an importance/uncertainty grid, pick two critical uncertainties as the axes of a 2×2 matrix, describe the four resulting futures against the NEB values in small groups, and compare them to draw strategic insights.","purpose":"We want to explore different plausible futures for a place, using key drivers and uncertainties to challenge assumptions, imagine alternative scenarios and identify actions that remain useful across different future conditions.","phase":3,"phaseName":"Co-Design & Scenario Building","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"1h30 - 2h30","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"Prepare in advance: \\n- a clear central question, for example: “How might this place evolve in the next 10 years?”\\n- a fixed time horizon, for example 2035\\n- 15–25 pre-written driver cards from the background research\\n- a blank importance / uncertainty grid\\n- a simple scenario canvas","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"- Start the workshop by explaining that scenarios are not predictions or preferred visions. They are plausible future situations that help participants think beyond the present, test assumptions and make better decisions under uncertainty. \\n- Keep the driver cards phase focused: the aim is not to brainstorm endlessly, but to agree on the most relevant forces of change.\\n- After each presentation, stress-test the scenario by asking: Is it plausible? Is it different from the other scenarios? Is it internally coherent? Does it challenge how we usually think? Does it help decision-making?","proTip":"","implementationTime":"1h30 - 2h30","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1. Read the central question and the time horizon. Keep them in mind throughout the activity. \\n2. Look at the driver cards prepared by the facilitator. Discuss whether they are clear, relevant and complete. Add missing drivers if needed. Think about social, environmental, economic, technological, political, cultural or governance-related forces that could shape the future of the place.\\n3. Place the driver cards on the importance/uncertainty grid. Use the horizontal axis to show how uncertain each driver is, from low to high uncertainty. Use the vertical axis to show how important each driver is, from low to high importance.\\n4. Look at the drivers in the top-right quadrant: these are both highly important and highly uncertain. As a group, choose two critical uncertainties. For each one, define two opposite poles. For example, “community stewardship” could range from weak to strong, while “public investment” could range from low to high.\\n5. Use these two uncertainties as the axes of a 2x2 scenario matrix. Together, describe the four possible futures created by their combination. Give each scenario an evocative working title.\\n6. Split into small groups. Each group works on one scenario and fills in the scenario canvas. Describe the future situation, what happened, which drivers shaped it, and what this future means in relation to the NEB values: Beautiful, Sustainable and Together.\\n7. Present your scenario briefly to the other groups. Listen to the other scenarios and check whether each one feels plausible, different from the others, internally coherent and useful for decision-making.\\n8. As a final step, compare all scenarios and identify strategic insights. Note which actions would be useful across several futures, which assumptions may be risky, and which early signals could show that one future is starting to emerge.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"08_Participation should start before key decisions are made and allow citizens to influence direction rather than react to predefined solutions.","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"top-10-design-principles-manifesto","name":"Top 10 design principles / Manifesto","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"08 Technique","shortDesc":"Grounded in the project vision, scenarios and NEB values, participants surface raw value statements, test each against a real trade-off to see if it resolves a genuine conflict, draft the survivors in a bold manifesto voice paired with a short \\"why\\", converge into a coherent set in plenary, and pilot-test them on real decisions before finalising.","purpose":"We want to draw up the 10 guiding principles for co-design: actionable, value-based statements that guide decision-making and ensure consistency across the project","phase":3,"phaseName":"Co-Design & Scenario Building","objectiveTags":["To self-govern"],"mode":"Hybrid","duration":"45-90 m","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"1/+10","suppliesRequired":"Markers or pens, \\nsticky notes, large white sheets of paper or a whiteboard to mark key words","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"- Use silent individual writing and dot-voting before open plenary debate, so ideas are assessed on content, not delivery.\\n- Keep the artifact visible throughout. Post-its on a wall, a shared live document, or a visual cluster map. \\n- Bring 2-3 example to calibrate tone before the group writes\\nA guiding principle should:\\n- be read as a stance, not a guideline: short, declarative, first-person plural (\\"We choose...\\", \\"We refuse...\\", \\"We design for...\\"). Aim for the boldness and precision of a manifesto rather than the safety of a generic value statement.\\n- Help resolve a genuine conflict between two competing goods (e.g., speed of delivery vs. depth of participation). \\n- Include the rationale, linked to the potential impact on people, communities, or the planet. This helps make the principle memorable, debatable, and defensible over time.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"45-90 m","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Ground the session in what the group already knows. Briefly recap the site-specific project vision, the scenarios developed so far, and the NEB values connected to them. Write each element on a sticky note.\\n2) In small mixed groups, ask: \\"What matters most to us in how we design this?\\" and \\"What would we never want to sacrifice, even under pressure?\\" Collect raw value statements on sticky notes (quantity over quality at this stage). Add a sticky note for every new element.\\n3) Guide the groups in reviewing, prioritising, and clustering what has emerged.\\n4) Ask each group to independently draft 2–3 candidate principles. \\n5) Ask the groups to share their principles in an open plenary session, cluster them into 2–4 thematic clusters so the set reads as a coherent system, not a checklist.\\n6) Run a confirmation round using the decision-making strategy you consider most appropriate. Use consent-based decision-making when time allows.\\n7) Monitor the use of the manifesto: can the group actually use them to decide? If a principle is never invoked, or two principles always pull in opposite directions with no clear priority, revise before locking the set.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"About Nature\\n1. Less Design Sprints, More Design Walks\\nIn a world where everything is rushed, we slow down to focus, connect and become one with Nature & our community. Creating better outcomes for your projects and the world.\\n2. Wander Aimlessly\\nPractice being okay with not synthesising and being in the unknown. Aimlessly wandering around in the “forest” allows for discovering the undiscovered. You start to see, hear, smell and feel the previously invisible. These might hold the answers to sustainable and regenerative questions.\\n\\nAbout Design\\n3. Co-create with nature, communities and economies\\nThe era of creating value solely for the end-user and your business is ending. Our creations need to benefit nature, communities, and economies for all to thrive and survive in the near and far future.\\n4. Prototype First, Design Second\\nStart prototyping at the beginning. Make it now and determine the blindspots in your design research. From what you uncover, you design a pathway forward.","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"lo-fi-prototyping","name":"Lo-fi prototyping","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"09 Techinque","shortDesc":"We want to make co-designed ideas tangible through quick, low-cost and low-fidelity representations that participants can see, handle, experience and discuss before testing them on real life.","purpose":"We want to make co-designed ideas tangible through quick, low-cost and low-fidelity representations that participants can see, handle, experience and discuss before testing them on real life.","phase":4,"phaseName":"Prototyping & Testing","objectiveTags":["To co-design"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"1h30 - 2h30","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"1/+10","suppliesRequired":"Cardboard, paper and foam board\\nMarkers, pens and sticky notes\\nTape, glue, scissors and cutters\\nString, fabric and modelling clay\\nSmall figures, blocks or movable objects\\nReused materials and everyday objects","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"- Treat the prototype as a learning tool rather than a final output. Its value lies in the evidence, shared understanding and design decisions generated through testing and revision.\\n- Follow the show, don’t tell rule: ask participants to demonstrate how the idea works instead of only explaining it.\\n- Build only the elements needed to answer the prototyping questions. Details that do not affect the test can remain undefined.\\n- Keep the prototype visibly unfinished and avoid unnecessary visual refinement. A polished result may discourage participants from questioning or changing it.\\n- Make sure participants can directly modify the prototype. Present it as a shared object that remains open to discussion, adaptation and improvement, not as a solution to approve.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"1h30 - 2h30","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Choose one or more promising ideas from the co-design process that would benefit from being made tangible.\\n2) Turn the idea into one or more clear prototyping questions: what do you want to learn from the prototype? Focus on the assumptions or uncertainties that could most strongly affect the solution.\\n3) Select the most appropriate method that can answer the prototyping questions:\\n- Sketches or storyboards to show the appearance, sequence or intended use of an idea.\\n- Mood boards or material samples to discuss atmosphere, identity, sensory qualities and aesthetic direction.\\n- Small-scale cardboard models to explore the organisation of spaces, objects, greenery, pathways and activities.\\n- Full-scale cardboard or tape mock-ups to test dimensions, reach, visibility, accessibility or relationships between objects.\\n- Desktop walkthroughs using maps, models and figurines to simulate how different people move through and use the proposed space.\\n- Roleplay or investigative rehearsal to test interactions, activities, services, rules, conflicts and responsibilities.\\n- Paper or simulated digital prototypes to test signs, booking systems, feedback interfaces or other digital components connected to the space.\\n4) Encourage participants to work rapidly and create alternatives rather than debating every detail before making something. When possible, develop two or more versions so they can be compared.\\n5) Ask participants to demonstrate how it works by showing what happens rather than only describing it.\\n6) Organise observations under simple categories such as strengths, problems, ideas and open questions. Change one element at a time where possible, test the revised version and compare its effects with the previous one.\\n7) Document what was learned and decide the next step.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"tactical-urbanism","name":"Tactical Urbanism","status":"Formatted for printing","typology":"10 Techinque","shortDesc":"We want to test possible spatial solutions directly on site through temporary, low-cost and reversible interventions, so that communities, designers and decision-makers can observe how people use them, collect feedback and refine ideas before making permanent changes.","purpose":"We want to test possible spatial solutions directly on site through temporary, low-cost and reversible interventions, so that communities, designers and decision-makers can observe how people use them, collect feedback and refine ideas before making permanent changes.","phase":4,"phaseName":"Prototyping & Testing","objectiveTags":["To self-govern"],"mode":"Offline","duration":"Not specified","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Without a facilitator","suppliesRequired":"","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"ND","proTip":"","implementationTime":"Not specified","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1. Start by selecting one or more ideas that emerged from the co-design process and that can be tested temporarily on site. These may include temporary seating, painted ground markings, movable planters, shade structures, pop-up play areas, signage, small event setups or temporary changes to circulation.\\n2. Define what the intervention is meant to test and for how long. For example, the test may last one day, one weekend, two weeks or one season, depending on the type of intervention, permissions, budget and maintenance capacity. Clearly communicate the duration to residents and users, explaining that the intervention is temporary, reversible and open to feedback. This helps build trust, manage expectations and make the evaluation process transparent.\\n3. Keep the intervention simple, reversible and proportionate to the time, budget and permissions available. Before installing the mock-up, check safety, accessibility, permissions, maintenance needs and responsibilities.\\nTo include the NEB values:\\nBeautiful: Co-create the intervention with residents, local groups, artists, designers or technical partners, so that it reflects the identity, atmosphere and sensory qualities of the place.\\nSustainable: When possible, build it with reused, recyclable or low-impact materials and integrate small-scale Nature-Based Solutions, such as planters, shade, permeable surfaces or rainwater elements.\\nTogether: Design the intervention so it can be used by people with different physical, cognitive, sensory and social needs, and make sure it is clear, welcoming and understandable for diverse users.\\n4. Combine physical and digital testing methods. The physical layer includes the temporary installation itself and direct observation of how people use it. The digital layer can include QR codes for feedback, short surveys, before/after photos, geolocated comments, or a simple usage observation dashboard to collect and organise evidence during the test.\\n5. During the test, observe how people actually use the space. Collect feedback through short interviews, observation notes, photos, counting, emotional mapping, QR forms, short surveys or simple feedback boards. Pay attention to who uses the intervention, who does not, what works, what creates confusion or conflict, and what unexpected behaviours emerge.\\n6. Include iteration moments during the test. Based on observations and feedback, define what can be adjusted immediately, what should be changed in the next version, and what should be tested again. Small changes may include moving furniture, changing signage, adding shade, adjusting access, improving visibility or simplifying instructions. Document each iteration so the team can understand what changed and why.\\n7. After the test, review the evidence with participants, technical partners and decision-makers. Identify what should be kept, changed, removed or developed further. The results can inform the next design iteration, support decision-making and help decide whether a temporary solution should become permanent, be adapted or be replaced by another option.\\n8. Use the results to discuss governance and stewardship after the test. If the intervention is successful, clarify who could maintain it, who could activate it, what resources are needed, and what responsibilities should be shared. The test can inform maintenance agreements, stewardship roles, community care practices or future management models.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"Superblock (Superilla), Barcelona\\nPiazze Aperte (Open Squares), Milan\\nSuperkilen Park, Copenhagen\\nPiazza dell'Immaginario, Prato\\nPrecollinear Park, Torino","requirements":"18_Fast, low-fidelity prototyping and iterative feedback loops should be used throughout the process","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":""},{"id":"public-feedback-update","name":"Public Feedback update","status":"Content ready","typology":"Tool 17 + event report document (online)","shortDesc":"We want to support transparent communication and manage expectations by making the relationship between community input and project decisions visible.","purpose":"We want to support transparent communication and manage expectations by making the relationship between community input and project decisions visible.","phase":5,"phaseName":"Consolidation, Governance & Learning","objectiveTags":[],"mode":"Offline","duration":"Not specified","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"- Complete the table after each significant engagement, testing or decision-making phase; do not wait until the end of the process.\\n- Use clear, non-technical language and remain faithful to what participants expressed.\\n- Do not report only the ideas that were accepted. Explaining constraints and rejected proposals is essential for transparency and trust.\\n- Distinguish between inputs that were not adopted and those that are still under review or outside the project’s scope.\\n- Group similar contributions without hiding conflicting views or concerns raised by less-represented participants.\\n- Revise the table when decisions change or new evidence becomes available.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"Not specified","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) After each significant engagement, testing or decision-making phase, use the Event Report Template and the Feedback log (both available on our website) to document what happened and decisions taken.\\n2) Group related contributions into a limited number of clear themes and transfer the main outcomes into the Public feedback update template.\\n3) Bring the latest version to every public event. Display it at the entrance, near the working materials or during the closing session, and briefly explain what has changed since the previous activity.\\n4) Publish the table on the project website or digital platform, as well as through other relevant communication channels. Update it whenever a decision, constraint or next step changes.\\n5) Where possible, link each update to visible evidence, such as revised drawings, prototypes, meeting summaries, photographs or decision documents.\\n6) Keep previous versions accessible so that participants can follow how contributions, decisions and project responses have evolved over time.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":"Add excel link: \\nAdd doc link:"},{"id":"stewardship-canvas","name":"Stewardship Canvas","status":"Content ready","typology":"Tool 18 + online log","shortDesc":"We want to make existing stewardship initiatives visible, identify new care needs, and connect individuals, groups and organisations willing to contribute to the long-term care and activation of the place.","purpose":"We want to make existing stewardship initiatives visible, identify new care needs, and connect individuals, groups and organisations willing to contribute to the long-term care and activation of the place.","phase":5,"phaseName":"Consolidation, Governance & Learning","objectiveTags":[],"mode":"Offline","duration":"Not specified","groupSize":"Not specified","budget":"Flexible","facilitatorRatio":"Not specified","suppliesRequired":"","expectedOutputs":[],"accessibilityNotes":"","usageTip":"- Present the templates as an invitation to contribute, not as a request for residents to replace public services or assume responsibilities that belong to municipalities, landowners or specialist professionals.\\n- Use concrete language and identify specific elements or activities rather than speaking in general terms.\\n- Distinguish clearly between a suggestion, an expression of interest and a confirmed commitment.\\n- Encourage different forms of contribution, including time, knowledge, skills or coordination.\\n- Connect new proposals to existing initiatives where possible.\\n- Check whether contributors need permissions, resources or support before an initiative becomes active.\\n- Do not treat empty spaces or unassigned needs as a failure; they highlight gaps in capacity or responsibility.","proTip":"","implementationTime":"Not specified","developmentTime":"Not specified","howTo":"1) Before each public event, review the online internal version (available on our website) and pre-fill the two columns with the initiatives currently being developed and the actors already taking care of them.\\n2) Leave the tool on sight during the event, at the beginning explain briefly what has changed since the previous activity, highlighting new agreements, ongoing initiatives and unresolved needs.\\n3) At the end of the activities, invite participants to add their name, group or organisation next to an existing initiative and describe how they could contribute to existing and elements that needs care.\\n4) Make clear that identifying a need does not make the person who proposed it responsible for addressing it.\\n5) After the event, review the contributions, contact potential contributors where appropriate, and transfer the relevant information to the online internal version.\\n6) Update the public template when an offer becomes a confirmed commitment, when a new actor joins, or when the status of an initiative changes.\\n7) Keep previous versions accessible so participants can follow how stewardship needs, contributions and commitments evolve over time.","budgetAdaptation":"","examples":"","requirements":"01_The scope of influence, decision rules, and use of inputs should be explicitly defined and transparently communicated","reference":"","printableUrl":"","onlineResources":"Add excel link:"}]`);export{e as s};
